[Music]  genes  genomes and dna these words have slipped  into our daily news cycles and our  awareness  but what they actually mean often  remains unclear  in these lessons we aim to give you not  just the explanation of what a genome is  but a real life perspective on why you  already care about genomics  perhaps in ways you didn't even realize  what is a genome a genome contains all  of the  information that a cell needs to develop  function and reproduce itself  and all the information needed for those  cells to come together to form a person  plant or animal genomes contain an  organism's complete set of genes  and even tinier genetic structures that  help regulate when and how those genes  are used  the ability to regrow a torn ligament  the clues that might predict the onset  of mental illness  the nutritional potential of crops and  even the history of life  itself are all encoded in genomes  genomics is a new science  it tries to understand all the genetic  material that we have  the genes as well as tiny structures  that regulate where a gene is turned on  how active the gene is and how it  contributes to overall function  to begin in genomics everything starts  with dna  dna is the fundamental building block of  all of life  dna is a complex molecule think of dna  as a set of letters that form sentences  and eventually these sentences form  paragraphs and these paragraphs form  whole books  so we have dna and then we have genes  that are made up of pieces of dna and  then as we assemble all the genes  together  we have our book of life the genome  genomics is very much  technology driven we have benefited from  an incredibly fast development of the  technology  the core of genomics lies in sequencing  it has been amazing to watch the  technology develop very rapidly over the  last 30 years  it's not that long ago that we started  with a very very tedious process that  was done in individual labs that took  hours and hours to obtain a sequence of  maybe 20 or 30 nucleotides or pieces of  the dna  now it's possible to sequence an entire  human genome which is three billion such  pieces  in a few hours we've gone through  several forms of technology  and we've also gone through changes in  the organization of the science as a  result  in the early days everything was done in  individual labs  contributing in small amounts to the  sequencing that we were able to analyze  collectively now we are moving more and  more as the technology advances to  centralized facilities with very  expensive equipment that able to handle  large amounts of dna  and perform amazing feats of sequencing  genomics is not just based on sequencing  technology  it has actually spawned a whole other  area which is called bioinformatics  since genomics gathered such huge  amounts of data it's impossible to use  conventional  statistical approaches and general data  organizing approaches to handle it all  as a result we have a new breed of  scientists who is expert in biology  expert in computation and to be able to  bridge the gap and give us the tools we  need to make  sense of the data these scientists  bioinformaticians  are incredibly essential to the whole  genomics enterprise

